menuconfig MESHTASTIC_RF
	bool "RF gain-path diagnostics and measurement"
	default n
	help
	  Tools for answering "is this gain setting actually helping?" with
	  evidence rather than folklore.

	  There are four amplifiers between the air and a decoded packet and three
	  layers that can supply a setting for them, so a configured value and an
	  effective value routinely disagree. `meshtastic rf` reports what the
	  radio was really told; the measurement half turns received frames into
	  distributions that two settings can be compared across.

	  Safe to enable in an ordinary node build: the report is pure readback,
	  and the measurement half only observes frames the radio already
	  delivered. Nothing here transmits.

	  See main/docs/rf-measurement.md.

if MESHTASTIC_RF

config MESHTASTIC_RF_PATH_REPORT
	bool "`meshtastic rf` — effective gain-path report"
	default y
	help
	  A single screen walking the signal chain in order — front end, receive,
	  transmit, health — reporting the value that is IN EFFECT rather than the
	  one that is stored.

	  The distinction is the reason this exists. `sx126x_rx_boosted_gain` is
	  seeded into the config store and pushed to the driver at every boot,
	  overriding the devicetree default, so neither of those two sources is
	  the answer on a provisioned node. And the driver only STAGES the value:
	  it reaches the chip on the next lora_config(), so a config that failed
	  leaves the radio 2-3 dB less sensitive than the configuration claims.

	  Costs no RAM and nothing on the receive path.

config MESHTASTIC_RF_HIST
	bool "RSSI/SNR distributions and per-peer frame rates"
	default y
	help
	  Accumulate every received frame into a distribution, and count frames
	  per peer.

	  This is the piece that makes a comparison possible at all. The firmware
	  already counts a great deal, and the NodeDB keeps each peer's LAST SNR —
	  but a single sample of a noisy quantity is not evidence, so nothing
	  today can answer "did that antenna help?".

	  Taps the radio receive callback after the scanner's off-preset gate and
	  before the software queue, so it sees frames the queue drops (the radio
	  demodulated them; a full queue is our limit, not the RF chain's) but not
	  frames from a sweep of other people's presets.

config MESHTASTIC_RF_PEERS
	int "Peer table entries"
	depends on MESHTASTIC_RF_HIST
	default 24
	range 4 512
	help
	  About 40 bytes each. The default fits comfortably on a 256 KB nRF52840
	  alongside everything else; raise it in a per-board overlay where there
	  is room.

	  Eviction is least-recently-heard and is COUNTED, so a full table reports
	  that its peer list is partial instead of silently pretending otherwise.

endif # MESHTASTIC_RF
